$article=['date'=>'2026-10-03','slug'=>'autism-in-girls-missed','tag'=>'Autism','category'=>'Autism Spectrum Disorder','breadcrumb'=>'Autism in Girls','meta_title'=>'Why Autism in Girls Is Often Missed Until the Teen Years','description'=>'Learn why autism in girls may be overlooked, how masking can hide support needs at school, and what parents can document before seeking an assessment.','h1'=>'Why Autism in Girls Is So Often Missed Until Adolescence','dek'=>'A girl can appear socially capable at school while using enormous effort to observe, copy and recover afterwards.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Autism in girls can be missed when adults look only for the presentation historically associated with boys. Some girls have fluent language, want friendships and learn to copy social behaviour. Their difficulties may become visible only when adolescent relationships grow more complex and compensation stops being enough.</p>
<h2>Why the familiar autism picture can miss girls</h2><p>Diagnostic criteria apply across genders, but the examples people recognise are often narrow. A girl&rsquo;s focused interest may look age-typical in subject while its intensity, rigidity or function goes unnoticed. Quiet withdrawal may be labelled shyness, and distress may be treated only as anxiety.</p>
<h2>What masking actually means</h2><p>Masking or camouflaging describes strategies used to hide or compensate for autistic differences. A child may rehearse conversation, imitate a socially confident peer, force eye contact, suppress movements or memorise rules that other children seem to absorb intuitively.</p><p>Not every girl masks, and autistic people of other genders may mask too. Research supports camouflage as one contributor to missed recognition, not a complete explanation for every delayed diagnosis.</p>
<h2>“She is fine at school” is only half the evidence</h2><p>Structured routines and learned scripts can make a school day look successful. At home, parents may see exhaustion, silence, tears, irritability or a strong need to retreat. That contrast does not prove autism, but it deserves curiosity.</p><p>Ask teachers about unstructured time, group work, friendship repair, changes in routine and the effort needed to complete apparently successful interactions, not only grades and classroom behaviour.</p>
<h2>Why adolescence can expose hidden strain</h2><p>Teen friendships rely more heavily on subtle language, shifting alliances and unspoken expectations. Academic organisation also becomes more demanding. Strategies that worked in primary school may no longer cover difficulties with sensory load, flexibility, executive functioning or social interpretation.</p>
<h2>What parents can document</h2><ul><li>differences between school presentation and after-school recovery;</li><li>friendship patterns, misunderstandings and reliance on one socially confident friend;</li><li>sensory responses, routines and distress around change;</li><li>the intensity and purpose of interests;</li><li>examples from early childhood, including play and communication.</li></ul>
<p>A comprehensive assessment should draw on developmental history, the young person&rsquo;s experience, information across settings and direct clinical observation. One polished conversation or screening score should not decide the outcome.</p>
<div class="safety-card"><p><strong>Avoid coaching a performance:</strong> help your daughter describe what social situations cost her, not how she thinks an autistic person is expected to behave.</p></div>
<h2>When to seek an assessment</h2><p>Consider an evaluation when lifelong patterns affect wellbeing, friendships, education or daily functioning. The purpose is to understand strengths and support needs, including anxiety or other coexisting concerns. Read more about <a href="/autism/autism-in-girls">autism in girls and women</a>.</p>
